  <uml:Package xmi:id="_18_4_1_e120334_1499698941481_944701_44554" xmi:uuid="028bc26c-6e90-4c64-9b70-dc2d083516e0" xmi:type="uml:Package">
    <ownedComment xmi:id="_18_4_1_8e001ed_1510136649872_483705_30935" xmi:uuid="d1e38435-6920-4a08-bc3d-6a9ad01ca18a" xmi:type="uml:Comment">
      <annotatedElement xmi:idref="_18_4_1_e120334_1499698941481_944701_44554"/>
      <body>This capability enables the representation of a wiring harness assembly design, consisting of: &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;connectors consisting of housing, backshell, contacts, inserts, seals, plugs and other auxiliary components;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;simple and complex contact templates that can be realized by families of connectors;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;single electrical or optical wires and cables with multiple wires, including shields;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;identification of terminal features at the end of wires and intermediate positions;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;identification of terminal features on various kinds of contacts, including connector contacts, lugs, terminals, splices and others;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;identification of intended connection purpose of a terminal feature, including crimp, solder, welding, push, screw, clamp an others;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;detailed join information of terminal features and how the joint is realized by crimping, soldering, and others;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;topological segment structure, consisting of connected vertices and edges with length, and paths through them;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;cross-sectional segment structure supporting coverings and protections, lacing and twisting of groups and sub-groups of wires, cables and bundles.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; The information kept in a wiring harness assembly design can be associated to: &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;2D geometry to produce the layout for a formboard in 1:1 scale;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;technical drawings for a stick-line representation that is not to scale;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;3D geometry by associating edges to curves and vertices to positions.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;br&gt; The figure below provides an overview of the capability. &lt;figure&gt;&lt;name&gt;Electrical_domainModel_overview.png&lt;/name&gt;Simplified overview of the AP242 electrical harness Domain model&lt;/figure&gt; &lt;br&gt; Key entities and associated relationships:&lt;br&gt; For the purpose of electrical wire harness, it is essential to categorize the Parts whether they are wires, cables, connectors, contacts, fixings and others. This helps preprocessors to manage the imported data and prepare for the target system.&lt;br&gt; A typical WiringHarnessAssemblyDesign consists of rigid elements such as connectors that are represented by a SingleOccurrence and flexible elements with a specified length such as wires, cables, protections that are represented by WireOccurrences, CableOccurrences and QuantifiedOccurrences respectively. This mixture of rigid and flexible elements is represented by an EdgeBasedTopologicalRepresentationWithLengthConstraint that is composed of VertexPoints with unspecified coordinates and Paths that are defined by EdgeBoundedCurveWithLengths where the exact geometry of the Curve is undefined and only the length is provided. A HarnessSegment links an EdgeBoundedCurveWithLength to a single or a group of flexible Occurrences: &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;either to a corresponding CrossSectionalPartShapeElement for the case that the edge is traversed by only one occurrence;&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;or otherwise to a CrossSectionalOccurrenceShapeElement of the WiringAssemblyHarnessDesign that represents the grouping of several occurrences for that edge.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt; The cross-sectional grouping of several flexible occurrences is done by a CrossSectionalGroupShapeElement or one of its subtypes: &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;CrossSectionalGroupShapeElementWithTubularCover,&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;CrossSectionalGroupShapeElementWithLacing,&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;TwistedCrossSectionalGroupShapeElement.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt; A HarnessNode links a VertexPoint to a rigid SingleOccurrence. There are different kinds of HarnessNodes:&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;an extremity_node is represented by a Vertex with a single Edge,&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;an intermediate_node is represented by a Vertex with two Edges,&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;a branch_node is represented by a Vertex with more than two Edges,&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;an external_node is defined elsewhere.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt; Because of the mixed rigid/flexible nature of a harness several 2D and 3D models might be needed (e.g. how the harness is built on the formboard as a 2D model and how the harness is installed in the vehicle as a 3D model). The mapping between the elements of the EdgeBasedTopologicalRepresentationWithLengthConstraint and the corresponding elements in a 2D or 3D model is done by TopologyToGeometryModelAssociation. The mapping between a 2D geometric model representing the cross section of a WireOccurrence, CableOccurrence or other any other QuantifiedOccurrence (by length) is done by GeometryToTopologyModelAssociation that maps the AxisPlacement representing the center of the underlying part to a Path or a single EdgeBoundedCurveWithLength of the EdgeBasedTopologicalRepresentationWithLengthConstraint. The combination of GeometryToTopologyModelAssociation and TopologyToGeometryModelAssociation allows to exactly know the centerline of the QuantifiedOccurrence (by length). In case that this QuantifiedOccurrenceLength is considered to be round, no further details for the 2D geometric model is needed. However, it is possible to provide detailed 2D cross section. &lt;br&gt; The connectors used for WiringHarnessAssemblyDesign are often composed of several pieces; some of them have terminals, others not. This document of ISO 10303 supports two different alternatives to represent the composition of modular connectors: &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;The connector can be represented as a hierarchical assembly with sub-assemblies, &lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;or all the pieces of the modular connector are directly used in the WiringHarnessAssemblyDesign in a flat assembly structure. In this case AssemblyShapeJoints are used to represent how individual pieces of a modular connector are joined. &lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; The detailed connectivity inside an electrical wire harness is represented by transport features, terminals and how they are joined. Typically a connector or a contact part defines fixed PartTerminals. They are replicated for every occurrence of a connector or contact as OccurrenceTerminals. &lt;br&gt; For all kinds of conductor including wires and cables, there are usually no PartTerminal defined. However, OccurrenceTerminals are defined for occurrences of these objects (cut in a certain length) so that they can be joined with other terminals. Conductors can often be contacted at arbitrary positions. Therefore, these elements have one or several PartTransportFeatures that are replicated for the usage of OccurrenceTransportFeature or subtype WireIdentification. OccurrenceTerminals can be defined on an OccurrenceTransportFeature. The subtypes WireOccurrenceTerminal and CableOccurrenceTerminal explicitly support the ends or intermediate positions. &lt;br&gt; AssemblyShapeJoint is used to state which OccurrenceTerminals are connected together (two or more). While AssemblyShapeJoint provides detailed connectivity information, PartConnectivityDefinition provides a high level statement (such as equipotential) for all the connected terminals within a PartView. &lt;br&gt; The generic shape of connector features such as slots, inserts, cavities, contacts and more can be described by ShapeFeatureDefinitions that can then be used to define the shape of several similar parts.</body>
    </ownedComment>
    <name>ElectricalHarness</name>
    <packagedElement xmi:id="_18_4_1_8db01ea_1515663031955_349528_50541" xmi:uuid="007a75b0-4dfc-4cc9-b9ce-3ff7fe4e7612" xmi:type="uml:Association">
